// Note: I'm including here the fixes in #18798 and #18489 that are unmerged to show how the final state might look
Reviewers of #13066 and #13225 asked for the catalog-side encryption code to be shared. Hive, REST and Glue (#18220) each copy the same per-table state: the key ID, data key length, keys, encryption manager and FileIO. The copies have already diverged.
This adds TableEncryptionState, owned by a catalog's table operations, and EncryptionUtil.checkKeyIdUnchanged. Each catalog still supplies the key ID from its own trusted source. Hive integrates them like this:
public FileIO io() { return tableEncryption.io(fileIO); }
public EncryptionManager encryption() { return tableEncryption.encryption(); }

// on refresh, for every table that exists in the catalog
tableEncryption.update(keyIdFromCatalog, dataKeyLengthFromCatalog, current());

// on commit
EncryptionUtil.checkKeyIdUnchanged(base, metadata);
tableEncryption.adoptForNewTable(metadata);

// in temp(uncommittedMetadata)
tableEncryption.adoptForNewTable(uncommittedMetadata);

Related to #18713
Summary
SparkTable.deleteWhere ignores spark.wap.id. A DELETE that Spark handles as
a metadata delete is therefore committed straight to the current branch instead
of being staged. This PR makes deleteWhere stage the commit and record the
wap.id, the same way the other two write paths already do. It touches Spark
3.5, 4.0, 4.1 and 4.2.
How Spark reaches this code
For DELETE FROM t WHERE <filter>, Spark first asks the table whether the filter
can be applied as a metadata-only delete (SupportsDeleteV2.canDeleteWhere). In
Iceberg that is true when the filter matches whole partitions or whole files, so
no data has to be rewritten. If it is true, Spark calls SparkTable.deleteWhere.
If not, it plans a row-level write, which goes through SparkPositionDeltaWrite
(merge-on-read) or SparkWrite (copy-on-write).
That gives three write paths for a DELETE:

Path
Class
Handled WAP before this PR?

Merge-on-read
SparkPositionDeltaWrite.commitOperation
Yes

Copy-on-write
SparkWrite.commitOperation
Yes

Metadata delete
SparkTable.deleteWhere
No

Where the bug is
File: spark/vX.Y/spark/src/main/java/org/apache/iceberg/spark/source/SparkTable.java
Method: deleteWhere(Predicate[] predicates)
This is the method before the change (Spark 4.1, lines 264 to 291):
public void deleteWhere(Predicate[] predicates) {
  Expression deleteExpr = SparkV2Filters.convert(predicates);

  if (deleteExpr == Expressions.alwaysFalse()) {
    LOG.info("Skipping the delete operation as the condition is always false");
    return;
  }

  DeleteFiles deleteFiles =
      table()
          .newDelete()
          .set("spark.app.id", spark().sparkContext().applicationId())
          .deleteFromRowFilter(deleteExpr);

  String writeBranch =
      SparkTableUtil.determineWriteBranch(
          spark(), table(), branch, CaseInsensitiveStringMap.empty());

  if (writeBranch != null) {
    deleteFiles.toBranch(writeBranch);
  }

  if (!CommitMetadata.commitProperties().isEmpty()) {
    CommitMetadata.commitProperties().forEach(deleteFiles::set);
  }

  deleteFiles.commit();
}
Nothing in it reads spark.wap.id or the write.wap.enabled table property.
deleteFiles.commit() runs without stageOnly(), so SnapshotProducer.commit()
takes the setBranchSnapshot branch and moves the branch head.
For comparison, this is what the other two paths do in their commitOperation:
if (wapEnabled && wapId != null) {
  operation.set(SnapshotSummary.STAGED_WAP_ID_PROP, wapId);
  operation.stageOnly();
}
What goes wrong
With write.wap.enabled=true and SET spark.wap.id = 'x':

DELETE FROM t WHERE <whole partition> runs through deleteWhere.
The delete is committed to main immediately. Readers see it before anyone
audited or published anything.
No snapshot has summary['wap.id'] = 'x', so
SELECT snapshot_id FROM t.snapshots WHERE summary['wap.id'] = 'x' returns
zero rows.
cherrypick_snapshot has no snapshot ID to work with.

The code never looks at the table format version or at deletion vectors, so this
also happens on v2 tables.
The fix
The block below is added in deleteWhere, after the branch is resolved and
before CommitMetadata is applied (Spark 4.1 and 4.2):
// honor spark.wap.id for metadata deletes so the change is staged rather than applied to the
// current branch
SparkWriteConf writeConf = new SparkWriteConf(spark(), table());
if (writeConf.wapEnabled() && writeConf.wapId() != null) {
  deleteFiles.set(SnapshotSummary.STAGED_WAP_ID_PROP, writeConf.wapId());
  deleteFiles.stageOnly();
}
On Spark 3.5 and 4.0 the field names differ and SparkWriteConf has no
two-argument constructor, so it reads:
SparkWriteConf writeConf =
    new SparkWriteConf(sparkSession(), icebergTable, Collections.emptyMap());
What each line does:

SparkWriteConf provides wapEnabled() (the write.wap.enabled table
property) and wapId() (the spark.wap.id session setting). These are the same
two values SparkWrite and SparkPositionDeltaWrite use, so all three paths
now share one definition of "WAP is on".
